// ir
// Pins the boxed-tuple ABI (ABI.md §1.1) at the IR level, because program output
// cannot see it. A tuple is laid out exactly like a class: elements in order,
// each aligned to its own size, body rounded up to 8, and every reference
// element listed in `ptrs` for the collector.
//
// Why this case exists as an `// ir` golden and not just a `// run` one: lowering
// an element at its default i64 width instead of its declared width still
// *prints* the right answers, because the stores run in increasing-offset order
// so each oversized store is repaired by the next one, and only the last spills
// past the body. That defect is invisible to stdout and plainly visible here as
// `const_int i64` feeding a `u8` slot. Assert on the emitted IR for layout work.
//
// #2426 removed the `field_get` reads this used to show: block-local
// store-to-load forwarding answers each one from the `field_set` above it, and
// these three same-base different-offset reads are exactly the case its rule 2
// (compiler/optstore.bit) exists for. The width claim above is unaffected — it
// is the STORE side, `field_set %3[4] = %1` with `%1 = const_int i32 70000`,
// that carries it, and those lines are all still here.
//
// #3622 then removed `main`'s two copies of those same boxes — the ones
// inlining pasted in, whose handles nothing reads once forwarding has answered
// every element from the stored value. `narrow` and `mixed` above keep theirs,
// and that is the half of this golden worth reading twice: an allocation whose
// result escapes through `ret` is NOT removable, so those two `gc_alloc`s plus
// their `field_set`s and `ret %3`/`ret %2` are what pins dead-allocation
// elimination to the cases it can prove. The width claim still rides on
// `narrow`'s stores, which the pass cannot touch.
fn narrow(): (u8, i32, u8) {
  return 200, 70000, 44
}

// A reference element must appear in `ptrs`, an untraced one must not — this is
// the entire precision contract the collector relies on (ABI.md §2).
fn mixed(): (string, i64) {
  return "s", 9
}

fn main() {
  let (a, b, c) = narrow()
  let (s, n) = mixed()
  print("${a} ${b} ${c} ${s} ${n}\n")
}
